course: dictionary
lesson: 10052
title: Strong words for fear
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Terrorise is a strong verb. To terrorise people is to frighten them very badly, again and again, often by threats or violence. The word tells you the fear is not a small shiver but a long, heavy fear. In «In Jaws one shark terrorises a whole town», the shark does not scare people once; it keeps the whole town afraid.
explain: Look at the words around it. Gangs, pirates or armies can terrorise towns, and a dragon can terrorise a village in a story. These are all frightening, cruel actions, so the clues point to fear caused by threats or violence.
explain: Sometimes the word is playful. A puppy can terrorise a cat by chasing it round the house, and nobody is really in danger. Ask yourself: is the situation serious, or is it a joke? The sentence tells you which meaning the writer means.
explain: In school, terrorising another child is bullying, and it must always be reported to a teacher or a trusted adult. In American English the spelling is terrorize, with a z, but the meaning is the same.
fact: Terrorise is a verb: terrorises, terrorised, terrorising.
fact: «Terrorise» means to frighten people very badly, again and again, often by threats or violence.
fact: Common phrases include «terrorise a town» and «terrorised by», as in «terrorised by a dragon».
fact: In «In Jaws one shark terrorises a whole town, but real shark attacks are very rare», the story is scary, but real shark attacks are very rare.
fact: Most of the 500 or so kinds of shark are smaller than a person, and people catch tens of millions of sharks every year.
exercise: «The pirates terrorised the coast for years.» What does terrorised mean here? | Played a game of chase with the villagers | Sailed along the coast to look at the view | Painted the houses bright colours | *Frightened the people very badly, again and again
exercise: Nadia says, «The puppy terrorised the cat, but it was only playing.» What kind of terrorise is this? | The meaning of tidying up a room | *The playful meaning, like chasing and bothering | The serious meaning, with threats and violence | The meaning of sleeping soundly
exercise: «Terrorising another child is bullying and must be reported.» What must you do if you see it? | *Tell a teacher or a trusted adult | Join in so you are not left out | Wait and see if it stops by itself | Keep it secret to be polite
test: «In the story, a dragon terrorises the village every night.» Which word is closest in meaning to terrorises? | Feeds | Visits | Greets | *Frightens
test: «The town was terrorised by a storm that lasted all week.» Which word shows that the people were badly afraid? | Week | Lasted | *Terrorised | Storm
exam: Which sentence uses terrorise in its serious meaning? | My cat terrorised my sock while I was reading | The class terrorised the spelling test and got full marks | The puppy terrorised the slipper with a happy wag | *The pirates terrorised the coast, threatening sailors and burning boats
